#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125845 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2=1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39893af9755488d142116370d451b7a3d92b334c27f983eb302492b0d516c41c.png)\n\nFirst, let's draw our constraint circle ($x^2+y^2=1$) and consider the level curves of $f(x,y).$ These level curves are ellipses of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2 = k.\n$$\n\nAs we increase the parameter $k,$ we get ellipses with larger and larger axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/d5/d59d566c9cf7e252884a1397eb4ac0c8f85b290790e9063be2ff6eb4a4aff04a.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $k,$ a level curve will touch the circle at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ for some value of $k.$ The value of $k$ corresponding to that level curve gives us the *smallest* possible value of $f(x,y)$ on our circle $x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nIncreasing $k$ even further, we'll get another value of $k$ for which the corresponding level curve touches the circle at the points $(0, \\pm 1),$ as shown below. This value of $k$ gives us the *largest* possible value of the function $f(x,y)$ on our circle.\n\n![](images/48/48f71f5aa237936be24ccba572fb650b0d477c252d8338549a33cfea4874cbad.png)\n\nNotice that the corresponding level curves touch the circle in both cases (the minimum and the maximum). So, the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ and the circle $g(x,y)=1$ have a common tangent at these points. This means that the *gradients* of both curves must be parallel at these points. Therefore, at the extreme points, we must have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is some real number. The number $\\lambda$ is called a **Lagrange multiplier**.\n\nWe can now use these observations to write down a general method for optimizing a function subject to a constraint." :tutorial/id #uuid "932667c3-77f7-56cb-ae08-dd6835e29462" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5109 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125872 :tutorial/content "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "906426bb-122c-5b43-a304-d63921ab20dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7750 :tutorial/title "The Method of Lagrange Multipliers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125894 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5ffe247-960a-5ed1-92ae-ae8c917cbefc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7881 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
